Article 19

The obligations and responsibilities of the Private Sector and Community Institutions as well as the Community as referred to in Article 16 letter b, letter c, are implemented in the form of community participation.  (1)

The form of community participation as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:  @)
a. preventing the occurrence of violence against women and children;
b. providing emergency assistance to victims;
c. providing information and/or reporting acts of violence against women and children to law enforcement or authorized parties;
d. protecting victims; and €. participating in the handling of victims of acts of violence;

The form of community participation as referred to in paragraph (2), is carried out responsibly in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

CHAPTER II

FORMS OF VIOLENCE

Article 5

Forms of Violence against Women and Children, include:
a. Physical violence;

Psychological violence;

Sexual violence;

Domestic neglect;
Exploitation;

Trafficking in persons; and/or
Other violence.

Article 3

The objectives of organizing the Protection of Women and Children Victims of Acts of Violence are to:
a. prevent all forms of violence against women and children;

provide protection and services to women and children victims of gender-based violence;
c. provide a sense of security to women and children victims of violence,d. restore the physical, psychological and economic condition of women and children victims of violence, ande. take action against perpetrators of violence against women and children.

Article 8

1. Sexual violence as referred to in Article 5 letter c, includes:
a. non-physical sexual harassment;

physical sexual harassment;

contraceptive coercion;

forced sterilization;

forced marriage;

sexual torture;

sexual exploitation;

sexual slavery; and
electronic-based sexual violence.

2. In addition to sexual violence as referred to in paragraph (1), sexual violence also includes:
a. rape;
b. indecent acts;
c. intercourse with Children, indecent acts against Children, and/or sexual exploitation of Children;

acts violating decency that are contrary to the will of the Victim;
e. pornography involving Children or pornography that explicitly contains Violence and sexual exploitation,f. forced prostitution,g. criminal acts of trafficking in persons intended for sexual exploitation,h. sexual violence within the scope of the household;
i. criminal acts of money laundering where the predicate offense constitutes sexual violence crimes; andj. other criminal acts expressly stated as sexual violence crimes as regulated in the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 17

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 16 letter a include:  a. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
b. implementing policies for the protection of women and children from acts of violence established by the government;
c. conducting cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, as well as educational institutions, health institutions, hospitals, and various non-governmental organizations concerned with women and children from acts of violence;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children, in accordance with regional financial capacity;
f. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children; andg. realizing a Child-Friendly Regency.

In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent establishes programs and activities for victim protection action in the Regional Action Plan and Key Performance Indicators in supporting the main regional programs as a basis for the Region in implementing the protection of women and children.  (2)

The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) is part of the RPJMD.  (3)

Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) are regulated by Regent's Decree.  (4)

Article 21

The Regional Government conducts empowerment and awareness to families, parents, and the community.  (1)
The empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by:  (2)
a. providing material on the prevention of acts of violence against women and children in both formal and informal education;

providing accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;

creating a friendly and safe work environment atmosphere for women;

building community participation and concern for the protection of women and children from acts of violence;

building and providing an easily accessible information system;

building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women and children; and g providing information, guidance and/or counseling and realizing a Child-Friendly Regency.

Article 4

The scope of the Organization of Protection of Women and Children Victims of Acts of Violence in this Regional Regulation includes:
a. forms of violence;

the rights of Women and Children, obligations and responsibilities;
organization of protection;
special protection of Children;

guidance and supervision;

cooperation and partnership;

monitoring, evaluation and reporting; and
funding.

Article 2

The Implementation of Protection of Women and Children Victims  of Acts of Violence is carried out based on the principles of:
a. o0 g  ™0 ® humanity;

justice and gender equality;  non-discrimination;

order and legal certainty;  openness;

protection; and the best interests of victims.

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

REGENT OF DOMPU,

Considering:
That women and children are very valuable assets to ensure the continuity of the nation's existence, therefore protection from all forms of violence and injustice is necessary;

. That protection is carried out to guarantee constitutional rights and the fulfillment of the rights of women and children to be free from violence and treatment that degrades the dignity of women and children;

That to provide direction and foundation to all parties involved in the implementation of protection for women and children, a regulation is needed that can guarantee legal certainty effectively and efficiently;

. That based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Implementation of Protection for Women and Children Victims of Violence;

