CHAPTER VI

SPECIAL PROTECTION FOR CHILDREN

Article 38

The Regional Government and other institutions are obliged and responsible to provide Special Protection to Children.

Special Protection for Children as referred to in paragraph (1), is provided to:
a. Children in Emergency Situations;
b. Children in Conflict with the Law;
c. Children from Minority and Isolated Groups;
d. Children who are Exploited Economically and/or Sexually;
e. Children who are Victims of Narcotics, Alcohol, Psychotropic, and other Addictive Substance Abuse, . Children who are Victims of Pornography;
g. Children with HIV and AIDS;
h. Child Victims of Kidnapping, Sale, and/or Trafficking;

Child Victims of Physical and/or Psychological Violence; l s Children with Disabilities, Child Victims of Sexual Crimes;

Child Victims of Terrorism Networks;

2e Children who are Victims of Stigmatization from Labeling . Child Victims of Abuse and Neglect; Children with Deviant Social Behavior, and Related to Parental Conditions.

3. Special Protection for Children is carried out through efforts to: a. rapid handling, including treatment and/or physical, psychological, and social rehabilitation, as well as prevention of diseases and other health disorders;
b. psychosocial support during treatment until recovery;
c. provision of social assistance for Children from underprivileged families; andd. provision of protection and accompaniment in every judicial process.
4. Special Protection for Children as referred to in paragraph (1) is provided at UPTD PPA that has been established with reference to established service standards.
5. Special Protection for Children as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented quickly, comprehensively and in an integrated manner.

Article 22

In order to carry out services and protection for women and children from acts of violence, the Regional Government establishes UPTD PPA The UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1), functions to carry out services:  (1)
a. public complaints;  (2)
b. victim outreach;

a0  ™0 case management;

temporary shelter; mediation; and victim assistance.

CHAPTER III

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 12

Women and Children Victims of Acts of Violence receive the following rights:
a. the right to have their dignity and worth as human beings respected;

the right to recovery;

the right to determine their own decisions;

the right to obtain information;

the right to confidentiality;

the right to social rehabilitation;

the right to complaint handling that is appropriate, comfortable, and according to needs;

the right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process;

the right to assistance; and
to receive continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

Article 23

1. (2) (3)  (1) In implementing protection for women and children from acts of violence, the Regional Government may also establish a task force.

The task force as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:  a. Task Force for the Prevention and Handling of Criminal Acts of Human Trafficking;
b. Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children;
c. Task Force for the Prevention of Child Marriage;
Further provisions regarding the position, organizational structure, duties and functions as well as working procedures of the task force as referred to in paragraph (2) are stipulated by Regent's Regulation.

Article 28

Forms of services for acts of violence experienced by Women and Children include: complaint services;  (1)
a. health services;
b. a0  ™o ™ legal aid;

submission of reports;

repatriation and social reintegration, rehabilitation, and assistance services.

2. In implementing the forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government cooperates with relevant regional apparatus/institutions.

Article 13

Children Victims of Acts of Violence in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 12 also receive special rights as follows:
a. the right to survival, growth, and development;

the right to basic population services;

the right to equal protection;

the right to be free from various stigma; and
the right to obtain freedom.

